                "Cross-border market entry modes exist along a continuous spectrum governed by a four-way trade-off: Financial Risk, Operational Control, Resource Commitment, and Potential Profit Return. Strategists categorize entry modes into four distinct operational classes:",
                "1. Exporting Modes (Lowest Risk, Lowest Control): (a) Indirect Exporting: Selling domestic goods to an export trading company or export management company located in the home country, who handles all foreign shipping and marketing. The firm incurs zero foreign capital commitment and zero foreign exchange risk, but possesses zero control over how its brand is positioned abroad; (b) Direct Exporting: Selling directly to independent foreign distributors or directly to foreign end-users via digital cross-border platforms. The firm captures higher margins and gathers market intelligence, but bears shipping logistics and foreign credit risk.",
